  "answer": "**Yes—under Manual practice, a general consent must still identify the applicant receiving it.** It need not identify that applicant’s trade mark or the particular goods and services claimed. The letter must meet the owner-name and signatory requirements for specific consent letters and should contain no conditions. [TMM/Part27/4/4/2/5] Those requirements include matching the consenting owner’s name to the database and having the letter signed by that owner or an authorised signatory. [TMM/Part27/4/4/2/3]\n\n**For formality issues, the Manual practice is:**\n- For one or two minor issues, the examiner should attempt telephone or email contact, request the information through online services, and retain a record of the contact. [TMM/Part10/8#1]\n- If the information has not arrived through online services within one week after contact, the examiner should issue a report if necessary. If other matters already require a report, include the formality requirements in it. [TMM/Part10/8#1]\n- Any response that will result in amendment of the application must be submitted through online services. [TMM/Part10/8#1]\n\n**Legislation limitation:** The Manual states that unmet formality requirements require rejection under section 33(3). However, the supplied Act extract contains only the opening words of that subsection, so it does not independently establish the statutory conditions for rejection. [TMM/Part10/8#1; TMA1995/s33(3)]"
